    "Acute hypotension in patients with obstructive HCM is a medical urgency. Maximizing preload and afterload, while avoiding increases in contractility or heart rate, is crucial for treating acute hypotension. Intravenous vasoconstrictors like phenylephrine can reverse this situation. Beta-blockade can be useful in combination with vasoconstrictors as it dampens contractility and improves preload by prolonging the diastolic filling period. Cautious use of low-dose diuretics may provide symptom relief but aggressive diuresis can be problematic. Introducing therapies in patients with HCM being treated for coexisting conditions should be done cautiously. Medications causing or worsening symptoms related to LVOTO should be avoided. Recommendations for invasive treatment of symptomatic patients with obstructive HCM include surgical myectomy and SRT performed at specialized centers.": "Obstructive HCM",
    "Severe and progressive pulmonary hypertension thought to be caused by LVOTO or associated MR, Left atrial enlargement with symptoms of AF, Poor functional capacity as shown by treadmill test, High LVOT gradients in young children and adults. ": "Patient selection and treatment strategies for obstructive HCM include determining the appropriateness of GDMT, SRT, or surgical myectomy. Surgical options are recommended for patients with severe symptoms or in those for whom surgery is contraindicated. Sub-sections include specific surgical indicators like severe pulmonary hypertension, left atrial enlargement, and elevated LVOT gradients."
